import json

questions = []

def add_q(qid, category, question, options, correct_idx, explanation):
    assert len(options) == 5, f"{qid} must have 5 options"
    assert 0 <= correct_idx < 5, f"{qid} index invalid"
    opts = []
    for i, opt in enumerate(options):
        opts.append({"text": opt, "is_correct": (i == correct_idx)})
    questions.append({
        "id": qid,
        "category": category,
        "question": question,
        "options": opts,
        "explanation": explanation
    })

CAT4 = "Action Potentials & Synaptic Neurotransmission"
CAT5 = "Neuromuscular Junction Disorders & Toxicology"
CAT6 = "Anesthetic Pharmacology & Neuromuscular Blockade"

print("Starting Part 2...")

# Q101 - Q115
add_q("inhouse_101", CAT4,
"A neurophysiologist records the electrical activity of a giant squid axon using an intracellular glass microelectrode. In the unexcited, resting state, the resting membrane potential across the axonal membrane is -70 mV. What primarily establishes and maintains this negative resting membrane potential?",
["High resting membrane permeability to K+ via non-gated leak channels and the electrogenic Na+/K+ ATPase", "High resting membrane permeability to Na+ and influx of chloride anions", "Complete impermeability of the resting axonal membrane to all inorganic ions", "Active transport of Ca2+ into the intracellular cytoplasm by SERCA pumps", "Electrogenic outward pumping of positive protons by ATP synthase"],
0,
"The negative resting membrane potential of excitable cells (-70 mV in neurons) is primarily established by two mechanisms: 1) The resting membrane is 50 to 100 times more permeable to Potassium (K+) than to Sodium (Na+) due to constitutive, open NON-GATED K+ LEAK CHANNELS. K+ diffuses down its chemical concentration gradient out of the cell, carrying positive charges out and leaving uncompensated negative proteins/anions behind inside the cell. 2) The electrogenic Na+/K+ ATPase continuously pumps 3 Na+ ions OUT for every 2 K+ ions pumped IN, maintaining the chemical gradients and directly contributing a net negative charge to the interior.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Resting membrane permeability to Na+ is very low; high Na+ permeability occurs during action potential depolarization.\n- The membrane is selectively permeable, not completely impermeable.\n- SERCA pumps Ca2+ into the sarcoplasmic/endoplasmic reticulum, not across the cell membrane.\n- Proton ATP synthases generate mitochondrial ATP, not resting neuronal potentials.")

