add_q("inhouse_116", CAT4,
"A dentist injects lidocaine into the inferior alveolar nerve to numb a mandibular molar before an extraction. The patient notes that pain and temperature sensation in her lower lip and teeth are lost rapidly within 3 minutes, whereas motor control of the jaw and deep touch sensation are preserved for much longer. Which biophysical principle governs local anesthetic action, and why are pain fibers blocked before motor fibers?",
["Lidocaine enters axons in an uncharged form, becomes protonated intracellularly, and blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels from the inside; small, myelinated and unmyelinated pain fibers (A-delta and C) are blocked before large motor fibers (A-alpha)", "Lidocaine binds exclusively to the extracellular surface of the channel; large fibers are blocked before small fibers", "Lidocaine activates GABA-A receptors on the outer myelin sheath", "Lidocaine irreversibly destroys Schwann cells", "Lidocaine blocks delayed rectifier potassium channels exclusively"],
0,
"Local anesthetics (e.g., lidocaine, bupivacaine) are weak bases. They cross the hydrophobic axonal lipid bilayer in their uncharged, non-ionized lipophilic form. Once inside the axoplasm, they become protonated into the active, ionized (cationic) form and bind to the inner pore vestibule of VOLTAGE-GATED SODIUM CHANNELS, with greatest affinity for the open and inactivated states (USE-DEPENDENT BLOCK). The sequence of clinical nerve blockade is: small myelinated fibers (B-fibers autonomic > A-delta pain/temperature) -> unmyelinated fibers (C-fibers dull pain/temp) -> large myelinated fibers (A-beta touch/pressure > A-alpha motor). Small fibers are blocked first because their critical length of axon needed to block conduction (2-3 successive nodes of Ranvier) is physically much shorter.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Lidocaine must cross the membrane and bind from the cytosolic/pore side, not exclusively extracellularly.\n- GABA-A receptors are not the primary target of local anesthetics in peripheral nerves.\n- Lidocaine does not destroy Schwann cells at clinical concentrations.\n- Potassium channels are not the primary target of lidocaine.")

